></div></div><p id=3D"e6e0" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b k=
o kp kq kr ks kt ku kv kw kx ky kz la lb lc ld le lf lg lh li eo bj" data-s=
electable-paragraph=3D"">In the same way that <a class=3D"af lj" href=3D"ht=
tps://www.scottbrady91.com/OAuth/OAuth-is-Not-Authentication" rel=3D"noopen=
er ugc nofollow" target=3D"_blank">OAuth is not authentication</a>, it <str=
ong class=3D"kn ew">also does not tell us what the user is allowed to do or=
 represent that the user can access a protected resource</strong> (an API).=
</p><p id=3D"9bbc" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko kp kq k=
r ks kt ku kv kw kx ky kz la lb lc ld le lf lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable=
-paragraph=3D"">Understanding what OAuth is not is just as important as kno=
wing what it is, in order to use it effectively. In this article, I=E2=80=
=99m going to discuss how OAuth does not include user authorization and why=
 user authorization rules should not live within your OAuth authorization s=
erver.</p><p id=3D"72fc" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko k=
p kq kr ks kt ku kv kw kx ky kz la lb lc ld le lf lg lh li eo bj" data-sele=
ctable-paragraph=3D""><em class=3D"lk">TL;DR: OAuth is not suitable for use=
r authorization. The fact that you have an access token that allows you to =
act on the user=E2=80=99s behalf does not mean that the user can perform an=
 action.</em></p><h1 id=3D"a997" class=3D"ll lm ev be ln lo lp lq lr ls lt =
lu lv lw lx ly lz ma mb mc md me mf mg mh mi bj" data-selectable-paragraph=
=3D"">Client Authorization (Delegation)</h1><p id=3D"11bf" class=3D"pw-post=
-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko mj kq kr ks mk ku kv kw ml ky kz la mm lc =
ld le mn lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">OAuth is an authori=
zation protocol, but maybe a better name for it is a delegation protocol. I=
t is a protocol that allows a client application to request permission to a=
ccess a protected resource (API) on the resource owner=E2=80=99s (the user=
=E2=80=99s) behalf.</p><p id=3D"3d9e" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph kl km=
 ev kn b ko kp kq kr ks kt ku kv kw kx ky kz la lb lc ld le lf lg lh li eo =
bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Let=E2=80=99s take a look at the authori=
zation process:</p><ol class=3D""><li id=3D"a058" class=3D"kl km ev kn b ko=
 kp kq kr ks kt ku kv mo kx ky kz mp lb lc ld mq lf lg lh li mr ms mt bj" d=
ata-selectable-paragraph=3D"">The client application asks the user if they =
can access a protected resource on their behalf (by redirecting the user to=
 the authorization server=E2=80=99s authorization endpoint, specifying exac=
tly what they would like to access (scopes))</li><li id=3D"4199" class=3D"k=
l km ev kn b ko mu kq kr ks mv ku kv mo mw ky kz mp mx lc ld mq my lg lh li=
 mr ms mt bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">The user identifies themselves=
 to the authorization server (but remember, OAuth is not authentication; yo=
u=E2=80=99ll need OpenID Connect to help you with that)</li><li id=3D"a221"=
 class=3D"kl km ev kn b ko mu kq kr ks mv ku kv mo mw ky kz mp mx lc ld mq =
my lg lh li mr ms mt bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">The user authorizes=
 the client application to access the protected resource on their behalf (u=
sing the OAuth consent process)</li><li id=3D"0898" class=3D"kl km ev kn b =
ko mu kq kr ks mv ku kv mo mw ky kz mp mx lc ld mq my lg lh li mr ms mt bj"=
 data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">The client application is issued an access =
token</li></ol><p id=3D"3055" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b=
 ko kp kq kr ks kt ku kv kw kx ky kz la lb lc ld le lf lg lh li eo bj" data=
-selectable-paragraph=3D""><strong class=3D"kn ew">An access token represen=
ts that the client application has been authorized by the user</strong>. Th=
e protected resource can look at the access token and say, =E2=80=9Cokay, A=
lice authorized this application to access these parts of my system on thei=
r behalf; I=E2=80=99ll let it through=E2=80=9D. However, <strong class=3D"k=
n ew">just because Alice said it was okay does not mean that Alice is autho=
rized to access the protected resource</strong>.</p><h1 id=3D"3937" class=
=3D"ll lm ev be ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly lz ma mb mc md me mf mg=
 mh mi bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Client Authorization vs. User Aut=
horization</h1><p id=3D"a07c" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b=
 ko mj kq kr ks mk ku kv kw ml ky kz la mm lc ld le mn lg lh li eo bj" data=
-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Let=E2=80=99s break that down, using a banking s=
ystem as an example:</p><ul class=3D""><li id=3D"58a2" class=3D"kl km ev kn=
 b ko kp kq kr ks kt ku kv mo kx ky kz mp lb lc ld mq lf lg lh li mz ms mt =
bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D""><strong class=3D"kn ew">Client authoriza=
tion:</strong> can this client application read Alice=E2=80=99s transaction=
 history?</li><li id=3D"7c27" class=3D"kl km ev kn b ko mu kq kr ks mv ku k=
v mo mw ky kz mp mx lc ld mq my lg lh li mz ms mt bj" data-selectable-parag=
raph=3D""><strong class=3D"kn ew">User authorization:</strong> can Alice vi=
ew the balance of account #123?</li></ul><p id=3D"c7ad" class=3D"pw-post-bo=
dy-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko kp kq kr ks kt ku kv kw kx ky kz la lb lc ld =
le lf lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">OAuth can handle clien=
t authorization by issuing an access token that Alice authorized. OAuth can=
not handle user authorization.</p><p id=3D"2900" class=3D"pw-post-body-para=
graph kl km ev kn b ko kp kq kr ks kt ku kv kw kx ky kz la lb lc ld le lf l=
g lh li eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Open Banking &amp; FAPI intro=
duce <a class=3D"af lj" href=3D"https://tools.ietf.org/html/draft-ietf-oaut=
h-rar" rel=3D"noopener ugc nofollow" target=3D"_blank">Rich Authorization R=
equests</a> (RAR), where a client application can ask the user to authorize=
 a specific transaction. But again, this is about the user consenting to th=
e transaction, as opposed to what the user is allowed to do.</p><h1 id=3D"4=
e3e" class=3D"ll lm ev be ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly lz ma mb mc m=
d me mf mg mh mi bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">User Authorization Rule=
s in your OAuth Authorization Server</h1><p id=3D"b107" class=3D"pw-post-bo=
dy-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko mj kq kr ks mk ku kv kw ml ky kz la mm lc ld =
le mn lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Hopefully, it=E2=80=99=
s now clear that an OAuth access token (or an OpenID Connect identity token=
 for that matter) does not represent the user=E2=80=99s permission to do so=
mething. But what about putting user-level authorization rules within your =
authorization server? After all, you already kind of have that with the use=
r authentication process.</p><h1 id=3D"c55b" class=3D"ll lm ev be ln lo lp =
lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly lz ma mb mc md me mf mg mh mi bj" data-selectabl=
e-paragraph=3D"">User-level authorization during authentication</h1><p id=
=3D"9b01" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko mj kq kr ks mk k=
u kv kw ml ky kz la mm lc ld le mn lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-paragrap=
h=3D"">This is where I am happy to give some ground. Confirming that the us=
er is authorized to use your system when they authenticate is perfectly acc=
eptable. For example, has the user been disabled by an administrator? Do th=
ey still qualify to use your services?</p><p id=3D"6210" class=3D"pw-post-b=
ody-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko kp kq kr ks kt ku kv kw kx ky kz la lb lc ld=
 le lf lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">If you decide that th=
e authenticating user is not authorized to use your system, you simply do n=
ot let them log in. These kinds of checks can also be performed during toke=
n generation or maybe as part of token validation (think of the introspecti=
on endpoint).</p><h1 id=3D"68a4" class=3D"ll lm ev be ln lo lp lq lr ls lt =
lu lv lw lx ly lz ma mb mc md me mf mg mh mi bj" data-selectable-paragraph=
=3D"">User-level authorization based on request</h1><p id=3D"0ebe" class=3D=
"pw-post-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko mj kq kr ks mk ku kv kw ml ky kz l=
a mm lc ld le mn lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">What breaks=
 this is when you try and add user-level authorization per client applicati=
on or protected resource. For instance, is the user permitted to use this s=
cope? Are they permitted to use this client application (while OAuth is not=
 authentication, you may see this check when using OpenID Connect)?</p><p i=
d=3D"fef4" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko kp kq kr ks kt =
ku kv kw kx ky kz la lb lc ld le lf lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-paragra=
ph=3D"">This would make your OAuth authorization process look like this:</p=
><ol class=3D""><li id=3D"a0b3" class=3D"kl km ev kn b ko kp kq kr ks kt ku=
 kv mo kx ky kz mp lb lc ld mq lf lg lh li mr ms mt bj" data-selectable-par=
agraph=3D"">The client application asks the user if they can access a prote=
cted resource on their behalf</li><li id=3D"1aa2" class=3D"kl km ev kn b ko=
 mu kq kr ks mv ku kv mo mw ky kz mp mx lc ld mq my lg lh li mr ms mt bj" d=
ata-selectable-paragraph=3D"">The user identifies themselves to the authori=
zation server</li><li id=3D"cb78" class=3D"kl km ev kn b ko mu kq kr ks mv =
ku kv mo mw ky kz mp mx lc ld mq my lg lh li mr ms mt bj" data-selectable-p=
aragraph=3D"">The authorization server decides if the user is authorized to=
 access the client or protected resource</li><li id=3D"0b54" class=3D"kl km=
 ev kn b ko mu kq kr ks mv ku kv mo mw ky kz mp mx lc ld mq my lg lh li mr =
ms mt bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">The user authorizes the client app=
lication to access the protected resource on their behalf</li><li id=3D"70a=
b" class=3D"kl km ev kn b ko mu kq kr ks mv ku kv mo mw ky kz mp mx lc ld m=
q my lg lh li mr ms mt bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">The client applic=
ation is issued an access token</li></ol><p id=3D"6bf2" class=3D"pw-post-bo=
dy-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko kp kq kr ks kt ku kv kw kx ky kz la lb lc ld =
le lf lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Adding another Access-=
Control List (ACL) to your authorization server can=E2=80=99t be that bad, =
right? After all, it is an <em class=3D"lk">authorization</em> server!</p><=
p id=3D"f58f" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko kp kq kr ks =
kt ku kv kw kx ky kz la lb lc ld le lf lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-para=
graph=3D"">In my opinion, this is <strong class=3D"kn ew">brittle and prone=
 to error</strong>. You are now back to relying on the fact that tokens wer=
e issued to represent that the user and the request have been authorized, w=
hich is not something OAuth was designed for and is a dangerous assumption =
to make.</p><p id=3D"4d2f" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko=
 kp kq kr ks kt ku kv kw kx ky kz la lb lc ld le lf lg lh li eo bj" data-se=
lectable-paragraph=3D"">Just as many attempts to use OAuth to represent use=
r authentication have failed, I=E2=80=99ve seen the same happen with user a=
uthorization too many times. The protocols just weren=E2=80=99t designed fo=
r it, and it=E2=80=99s just too easy to get wrong, especially when your tok=
en issuer is also providing SSO, issuing tokens without user interaction.</=
p><p id=3D"8790" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko kp kq kr =
ks kt ku kv kw kx ky kz la lb lc ld le lf lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-p=
aragraph=3D"">You are likely using a framework or out-of-the-box solution a=
s your OAuth authorization server, which will want to follow the OAuth spec=
ification and issue a token to the authorized client application. <strong c=
lass=3D"kn ew">You=E2=80=99ll spend your time fighting the design of OAuth<=
/strong>, and if you make a single mistake or misunderstand how your framew=
ork of choice works, then the authorization server will kick in and issue t=
he token as the user and client have requested, just like it is meant to.</=
p><p id=3D"4cad" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko kp kq kr =
ks kt ku kv kw kx ky kz la lb lc ld le lf lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-p=
aragraph=3D"">I know I=E2=80=99m blurring the lines between OAuth and OpenI=
D Connect a little here, but here=E2=80=99s a Stack Overflow question that =
I think highlights the kind of bizarre scenario you could find yourself in:=
</p><blockquote class=3D"na nb nc"><p id=3D"39ef" class=3D"kl km lk kn b ko=
 kp kq kr ks kt ku kv mo kx ky kz mp lb lc ld mq lf lg lh li eo bj" data-se=
lectable-paragraph=3D""><em class=3D"ev">=E2=80=9CI am looking [for] a way =
for [my authorization server] to invalidate the submitted token if the user=
 doesn=E2=80=99t have access to the application in a single go, even though=
 the challenged token might be valid if it is submitted by other applicatio=
n which the user has access to=E2=80=9D</em></p></blockquote><p id=3D"4e3c"=
 class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko kp kq kr ks kt ku kv kw k=
x ky kz la lb lc ld le lf lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Wh=
ile you initially wanted to have all of your user authorization rules live =
within your central OAuth authorization server, you will likely end up dupl=
icating these checks into your client applications and APIs to solve these =
kinds of problems. You are probably going to code yourself into a corner.</=
p><h1 id=3D"6d99" class=3D"ll lm ev be ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly =
lz ma mb mc md me mf mg mh mi bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">User Permi=
ssions in your OAuth Authorization Server</h1><p id=3D"fc73" class=3D"pw-po=
st-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko mj kq kr ks mk ku kv kw ml ky kz la mm l=
c ld le mn lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">This is where mos=
t of my customers end up after understanding that user-level authorization =
is not part of the OAuth protocol and that it is not an easy fit within the=
ir OAuth authorization server. But the desire to write all of their authori=
zation logic and user permissions in a single place still exists, which is =
understandable, especially when they have already achieved that with authen=
tication and API access by using an SSO solution that implements both OAuth=
 and OpenID Connect.</p><p id=3D"f345" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph kl k=
m ev kn b ko kp kq kr ks kt ku kv kw kx ky kz la lb lc ld le lf lg lh li eo=
 bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">So, what is the solution? Role-Based Ac=
cess Control (RBAC)? Stuff tokens full of user permissions?</p><p id=3D"650=
0" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko kp kq kr ks kt ku kv kw=
 kx ky kz la lb lc ld le lf lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">=
Well, that is a story for another day and well outside of this OAuth story.=
 I recommend checking out Dom=E2=80=99s excellent article on how <a class=
=3D"af lj" href=3D"https://leastprivilege.com/2016/12/16/identity-vs-permis=
sions/" rel=3D"noopener ugc nofollow" target=3D"_blank">identity and permis=
sions don=E2=80=99t mix within an SSO solution</a>. For solutions, take a l=
ook at how <a class=3D"af lj" href=3D"https://www.identityserver.com/docume=
ntation/enforcer/alfa/IntroducingAlfa/" rel=3D"noopener ugc nofollow" targe=
t=3D"_blank">authorization languages are evolving</a> with ALFA and how you=
 can create authorization policies in a single place that are readable by n=
on-developers.</p><h1 id=3D"c14f" class=3D"ll lm ev be ln lo lp lq lr ls lt=
 lu lv lw lx ly lz ma mb mc md me mf mg mh mi bj" data-selectable-paragraph=
=3D"">The Random Stuff</h1><p id=3D"a9b9" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph k=
l km ev kn b ko mj kq kr ks mk ku kv kw ml ky kz la mm lc ld le mn lg lh li=
 eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">This article would not be complete w=
ithout collating some of the weird and wonderful loaded questions I have re=
ceived on Stack Overflow or my blog. These are often followed by a =E2=80=
=9Cgotcha=E2=80=9D or a =E2=80=9Ccheckmate=E2=80=9D, or sometimes even a pe=
rsonal insult. But I=E2=80=99ll let your imagination come up with those.</p=
><h1 id=3D"7718" class=3D"ll lm ev be ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly l=
z ma mb mc md me mf mg mh mi bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">=E2=80=9CTh=
en why is it called an authorization code?=E2=80=9D</h1><p id=3D"1fe5" clas=
s=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko mj kq kr ks mk ku kv kw ml ky =
kz la mm lc ld le mn lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">The aut=
horization code represents the user authorizing the client application on t=
heir behalf. Again, that is client authorization, not permission to add use=
r authorization.</p><h1 id=3D"8c4f" class=3D"ll lm ev be ln lo lp lq lr ls =
lt lu lv lw lx ly lz ma mb mc md me mf mg mh mi bj" data-selectable-paragra=
ph=3D"">=E2=80=9CThen why do people return 401 Unauthorized when you don=E2=
=80=99t have a token?=E2=80=9D</h1><p id=3D"b30f" class=3D"pw-post-body-par=
agraph kl km ev kn b ko mj kq kr ks mk ku kv kw ml ky kz la mm lc ld le mn =
lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Naming is hard. In this use =
case, it would be nice to have named this status code =E2=80=9CUnauthentica=
ted=E2=80=9D, but at least <a class=3D"af lj" href=3D"https://tools.ietf.or=
g/html/rfc7235#section-3.1" rel=3D"noopener ugc nofollow" target=3D"_blank"=
>RFC 7235</a> requires that you include a <code class=3D"cw nd ne nf ng b">=
WWW-Authenticate</code> header alongside this status code.</p><h1 id=3D"30f=
7" class=3D"ll lm ev be ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw lx ly lz ma mb mc md =
me mf mg mh mi bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">=E2=80=9CThen why is it c=
alled the Authorization header?=E2=80=9D</h1><p id=3D"a6f8" class=3D"pw-pos=
t-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko mj kq kr ks mk ku kv kw ml ky kz la mm lc=
 ld le mn lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Again, naming is h=
ard. Basic Authentication also uses the Authorization header, but that does=
n=E2=80=99t mean the user has full access or is even allowed to use the app=
lication. You still have to check if the user is allowed to perform an acti=
on.</p><h1 id=3D"80f5" class=3D"ll lm ev be ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv lw l=
x ly lz ma mb mc md me mf mg mh mi bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">=E2=
=80=9CThen what are scopes for?=E2=80=9D</h1><p id=3D"7c28" class=3D"pw-pos=
t-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko mj kq kr ks mk ku kv kw ml ky kz la mm lc=
 ld le mn lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Scopes represent a=
 level of access that a client application is asking for from the user. For=
 instance, a scope called =E2=80=9Cemail.read=E2=80=9D might mean =E2=80=9C=
can I read your emails?=E2=80=9D, or =E2=80=9Cemail.send=E2=80=9D might mea=
n =E2=80=9Ccan I send emails on your behalf?=E2=80=9D. Check out Auth0=E2=
=80=99s article on <a class=3D"af lj" href=3D"https://auth0.com/blog/on-the=
-nature-of-oauth2-scopes/" rel=3D"noopener ugc nofollow" target=3D"_blank">=
the nature of OAuth scopes</a>.</p><p id=3D"2117" class=3D"pw-post-body-par=
agraph kl km ev kn b ko kp kq kr ks kt ku kv kw kx ky kz la lb lc ld le lf =
lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">This is a fairly common mist=
ake to make when starting out with OAuth, so I=E2=80=99ll forgive you for t=
his one.</p><h1 id=3D"3b2a" class=3D"ll lm ev be ln lo lp lq lr ls lt lu lv=
 lw lx ly lz ma mb mc md me mf mg mh mi bj" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">=
=E2=80=9CI still don=E2=80=99t believe you! Everyone does this!=E2=80=9D</h=
1><p id=3D"d971" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph kl km ev kn b ko mj kq kr =
ks mk ku kv kw ml ky kz la mm lc ld le mn lg lh li eo bj" data-selectable-p=
aragraph=3D"">Well, that=E2=80=99s just, like, your opinion, man. But it is=
n=E2=80=99t OAuth.</p></div></div></div><div class=3D"ab ca nh ni nj nk" ro=
